"# Appendix E — Signature cryptography (engineering literacy, not a Motoi feature)

The other dialect signs module artifacts so consumers can verify who published what. **Motoi signs nothing** — it has no module artifacts, no registry, and no key store. Every verb in this area — `module-signature`, `module/keygen`, `module/sign`, `module-verify`, `trust-add!`, `trust-remove!`, `trust-list`, `trusted?`, `module/rotate-key`, `module-signatures` — is `error: unbound symbol` in Motoi. This appendix is kept as *engineering literacy*: it is genuinely useful to understand how code signing works, even though Motoi implements none of it. Chapter 6 covers the same boundary in the chapter body; this appendix goes one level deeper into the crypto concepts.

## The algorithm (a literacy note)

Signing schemes like Ed25519 have three good properties worth knowing whatever language you use:

- **Fast** — signing and verification complete in microseconds.
- **Small** — signatures are 64 bytes; public keys are 32 bytes.
- **Sound** — no known practical attacks.

This is background knowledge. Motoi provides no Ed25519 verb and no key generation; there is nothing here to call.

## The signature record — does not exist

The other dialect's `(module-signature 'manners)` returned a record with `:algorithm`, `:author`, `:public-key`, `:signature`, `:hash-signed`, and `:signed-at`. **Motoi binds no `module-signature`** — it is `unbound`, because there are no modules and no signatures. The record shape is the other dialect's.

## What would get signed — nothing, here

In a signing system the signature covers the module's *content hash*, so signing and verification are fast and any content change invalidates the signature. Motoi has no module content hashes (Chapter 12 covers what identity *does* mean here: `eq?`/`equal?` on values), so there is nothing to hash-and-sign.

## Key generation — no verb

`(module/keygen \"a-friend\")` created a keypair in `~/.motoi/keys/` in the other dialect. **Motoi has no `module/keygen`** and no key directory. There is no author identity to generate keys for.

## Signing — no verb

`(module/sign 'manners)` read source, hashed it, and signed the hash. **Motoi has no `module/sign`.** Sharing code in Motoi is sharing source (Chapter 14); its integrity is the integrity of the file you were handed, verified by reading it, not by a signature.

## Verification — no verb

`(module-verify 'manners)` recomputed the hash and checked the signature. **Motoi has no `module-verify`.** The Motoi way to check that shared code does what it claims is to read it and run it.

## Trust decisions — no verbs

`trust-add!`, `trust-remove!`, `trust-list`, and `trusted?` maintained a per-user trust list in the other dialect. **Motoi binds none of them.** There is no author identity to trust or distrust, because there is no publishing identity.

## Key rotation and multi-author signing — no verbs

`module/rotate-key` and multi-signature `module-signatures` handled compromised keys and co-authorship in the other dialect. Motoi has neither, for the same root reason: no keys, no signatures, no module artifacts.

## What Cortex can record

The other dialect logs `module-verified` events you can query. Motoi has no such event. If you want to *note* that you reviewed some shared code and judged it sound, Cortex is real and you write the fact yourself:

```scheme
(cortex/write 'reviewed 'greet-source-ok)
(cortex/recall 'reviewed)
;; ⇒ a memory record whose :data is greet-source-ok
```

That is the honest Motoi shape of \"I checked this\": a fact you chose to record after reading the source, not a cryptographic verification event.

## Try this

Confirm that `module-signature`, `module/sign`, and `module-verify` are all `error: unbound symbol` (there is no signing here). Then record a review fact with `cortex/write` and read it back (above) — the Motoi way of tracking that you vetted some shared source, done by hand rather than by crypto.")
